{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788287935,"nonce":"C513weOcEMzhpzpszKK5x0Nxcxg=","message":"THIS ISSUE'S THESIS REPRODUCED LIVE, and the three connection rules a tiling\ndesign has to honour — all verified against the decompiled 1.6 source and then\nmeasured on a bench. Session 18, the M2 run.\n\n## What happened\n\n`power-room` (7x7) and `freezer-kitchen` (11x6) were placed on the same map by\ntwo `place-layout` calls, each sited by `find-rect` on clear ground, about ten\ncells apart. Both built clean. `digest.power` then read:\n\n    gen_w 1000, draw_w 0, nets 1, nets_with_generator 1\n\n**Zero draw.** The generator ran, the batteries charged, and the two coolers in\nthe other building were on a transmitter group with no power source attached to\nit. Neither template's conduit stub reached the other, because nothing had asked\nthem to. That is this issue's title — \"so layouts tile instead of landing as\nislands\" — happening exactly as written, and it is worth recording that it was\nNOT caught by any instrument: Evan caught it by looking at the screen and asking\nhow the generator was supposed to power the coolers.\n\nFixed by hand with a 19-cell `PowerConduit` trunk. `draw_w` 0 -\u003e 40 W.\n\n**The fix needed no new capability**, which is the useful part: the trunk was\nwritten as a throwaway rectangular IR grid with `PowerConduit` on the path cells\nand `.` everywhere else, and sent through `place-layout` like any other layout.\nBanked at `accept/runs/s18-20260901/trunk.ir.json`. Whatever tiling ends up\nemitting, a conduit run between two modules is expressible in the IR that exists.\n\n## THE THREE RULES\n\n**1. Transmitters join a net only by ADJACENCY.** Two conduit runs ten cells\napart are two `PowerNet`s no matter how close. This is the whole islands problem\nand there is no radius that rescues it.\n\n**2. CONNECTORS are different — they reach radius 6.**\n`RimWorld/PowerConnectionMaker.BestTransmitterForConnector` opens\n`CellRect cellRect = CellRect.SingleCell(connectorPos).ExpandedBy(6).ClipInsideMap(map);`\nand scans that 13x13 box for the NEAREST transmitter with `TransmitsPowerNow` and\n`allowWireConnection`. So a cooler five cells from a trunk connects itself with no\nconduit branch at all. **A tiling design that runs conduit to every consumer is\nover-building**; it only has to get a trunk within 6 of them. `freezer-kitchen`'s\nown spine already relies on this — its coolers sit 2-3 cells off the spine and\nconnect fine.\n\n**3. Generators and batteries ARE transmitters, and that has two consequences.**\n`Data/Core/Defs/ThingDefs_Buildings/Buildings_Power.xml` gives `WoodFiredGenerator`\n`\u003ctransmitsPower\u003etrue\u003c/transmitsPower\u003e` under its `CompProperties_Power`.\nSo (a) they bridge conduit groups themselves — the power room's stub at [128,129]\nand its two hidden conduits at [131..132,129] read as ONE net despite a two-cell\ngap, because the 2x2 generator sits in that gap and transmits; and (b) a conduit\nCANNOT be placed under them, refused by\n`RimWorld/GenConstruct.CanPlaceBlueprintOver`:\n\n    if (newThingDef.EverTransmitsPower \u0026\u0026 oldDef.EverTransmitsPower) return false;\n\n(`Verse/ThingDef.EverTransmitsPower` is `comps.Any(c =\u003e c is CompProperties_Power\n{ transmitsPower: not false })`.) A router that plans a straight line through a\ngenerator gets a refusal — correctly; our gate matched vanilla's exactly on this,\nnothing to fix mod-side. It should plan around, or better, recognise that it does\nnot need to route through a thing that is already a wire.\n\n## What this suggests for the module grid, without deciding it\n\n- A module's contract probably includes **where its trunk stub leaves the\n  footprint**, on the grid boundary, so two adjacent modules meet by construction\n  rather than by a router. The current templates each carry an internal spine that\n  stops at their own wall.\n- Rule 2 means the grid needs a trunk within 6 of every consumer, not a branch to\n  each — a materially cheaper geometry than it looks.\n- Rule 3 means a module containing a generator or battery already transmits\n  across itself, so the stub does not have to be continuous through it.\n- Non-contiguity inside a single template is invisible today. `power-room`'s\n  layer-1 conduits are `[128,129]` and `[131..132,129]` with a two-cell gap, and\n  it works ONLY because the generator happens to fill the gap. Move the generator\n  one cell and that template silently splits into two nets with no diagnostic\n  anywhere. **A template-level connectivity check is worth having**, and it is\n  cheap: flood-fill the transmitter cells, then verify every power-consuming\n  element is within 6 of the resulting group.\n\n## Suggested acceptance addition\n\n- Two adjacent modules from the grid, placed by two separate `place-layout` calls\n  with no hand-run conduit between them, land on ONE `PowerNet` — measured as\n  `digest.power.draw_w` rising off zero, not asserted from geometry.\n- The connectivity check above refuses (or at minimum reports) a template whose\n  own consumers cannot reach its own trunk, demonstrated against a deliberately\n  broken copy of `power-room` with its generator moved.\n\nCross-refs: `accept/runs/s18-20260901/` has the fixture, the trunk IR and the\nrender; `261f2e9` is the other half of making `freezer-kitchen` gradeable (the\ncoolers are powered now and still cannot be told to freeze).","files":null}]}